// @emit-error the exported generic function `id` is never instantiated
// An EXPORTED `<T>` generic that NO call site reaches is a clean emit REJECT — it used
// to be a silently-wrong wasm ABI, live since #552 and filed by #1257.
//
// #552's prune replaces an un-instantiated generic template with a no-op `() -> void`
// stub (valid dead code — nothing can reference it, since instantiation is the only way
// to reach a `<T>` decl). The stub KEEPS the declaration's name, and `emitExportSection`
// resolves the ABI by name, so this file used to build rc 0 and publish `id` with the
// functype `() -> void` — 34 bytes total. An importer calling `id(1)` gets `undefined`:
// the JS/wasm boundary drops the surplus argument, so nothing traps and nothing is
// reported. A SILENT WRONG ANSWER, the worst outcome class alongside invalid wasm.
//
// Why a reject rather than some concrete default: wasm has no generics, so an export
// names exactly ONE functype, and an un-instantiated `<T>` supplies no shape to pick —
// `T` could be i32, string, or a struct, which do not share a valtype. Nor can an
// external consumer supply one: VL's `import` MERGES the module graph before the
// monomorphizer runs, so every instantiation of an exported generic is an in-module call
// site by the time the prune reads the slot (see `modules/reexport-generic-called/`,
// which re-exports a dependency's generic AND calls it — that still builds and exports
// the concrete `(i32) -> i32`). The only remaining consumer is a wasm host, and a host
// cannot instantiate anything.
//
// The twin that must NOT move is `functions/exported-generic-called.vl` — the same
// declaration with one call site, which instantiates and exports its concrete shape.
export function id<T>(x: T): T { return x }

print(1)
